# Technology Scout Brief — Quiet-by-Design Terminal Technology for MWAA

Run: `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
Prepared: 2026-08-07
Lens: what is on the market, what is proven in airport deployment, what still needs
custom engineering, and what a MWAA design standard actually has to specify to be
buildable, testable, and enforceable.

The run brief warns against "recommending technologies or acoustic products before
defining the required performance and operating model." I take that seriously: this
brief is a scan of the technology stack organized around the performance targets a
MWAA standard would set, not a product catalog. Every category below is described
in terms of what it does to the measurable acoustic and information environment,
what standard governs it, and what is genuinely off-the-shelf in 2026 versus what
still requires bespoke integration.

---

## 1. Taxonomy: what "quiet-by-design technology" actually is

A useful taxonomy separates the stack into four layers. The failure mode of most
"quiet airport" projects is buying products from one layer without specifying the
others.

**Layer 1 — Room acoustics and finishes.** Absorption (NRC/SAA-rated ceilings,
wall panels, carpet), diffusion, HVAC and baggage-system source control, and
partition design. These set the ambient sound level (measured as NC or RC) and the
mid-frequency reverberation time (T30/RT60) that every downstream system inherits.
ACRP Research Report 175 documents that background noise in the surveyed terminals
ranged from roughly NC 45 to NC 60 and RT from about 1.2 to 3.5 seconds — a range
across which the same loudspeaker will produce Speech Transmission Index (STI)
readings that differ by 0.2 or more, i.e., the difference between "intelligible"
and "code-failing." [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrp0715/wp-content/themes/acrp-child/documents/201/original/acrp_r175.pdf]

**Layer 2 — Voice notification and PA.** Amplifiers, DSP, zone control,
loudspeaker density and directivity, plus the emergency voice-alarm layer required
by NFPA 72 and (where relevant) EN 54-16 certified equipment. In 2026 this layer
is dominated by IP-audio platforms (Q-SYS, Biamp Tesira, Bosch Praesensa, Bose
ControlSpace) that make fine-grained zoning trivial in software once loudspeaker
coverage is right in hardware. What is still hard is the loudspeaker layout: an
open-plan concourse under a 30-foot ceiling with 2.5-second RT will not become
intelligible by adding zones, only by adding sources with tighter directivity.

**Layer 3 — Visual and digital information.** Flight information display systems
(FIDS), dynamic wayfinding, gate-side monitors, "silent TV" strategies, and
digital signage feeds. Quiet-airport implementations from Helsinki, Schiphol, and
London City all rest on the same operating premise: passengers get flight status
from screens and the app, not from ceiling speakers. [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]
[Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]

**Layer 4 — Personal and assistive channels.** Airline and airport mobile apps
with push notifications, Bluetooth beacons for gate proximity, hearing loops
(induction) for hearing-aid users, and — the emerging one — Bluetooth LE Audio
Auracast broadcasts. Auracast lets a gate PA broadcast directly to any compatible
hearing aid, earbud, or phone inside the coverage bubble, replacing the induction
loop's copper wire with a software-defined coverage zone. [Source: https://www.bluetooth.com/auracast/assistive-listening/]

The taxonomy matters because MWAA's design manual can only enforce what it can
measure. Layer 1 and Layer 2 have mature ISO/IEC/NFPA metrics. Layer 3 has
industry-standard uptime SLAs. Layer 4 accessibility is largely governed by ADA
Section 706 for assistive listening plus airline ACAA obligations. The standard
should be written against those metrics, not against product names.

