## 2. Key findings

- **The reference document already exists.** ACRP Research Report 175, *Improving
  Intelligibility of Airport Terminal Public Address Systems* (TRB / Wilson Ihrig,
  2017), is the closest thing the industry has to a written quiet-airport
  engineering baseline for U.S. airports. It surveys real terminal acoustics,
  documents the STI/RT/NC relationships, and prescribes design and commissioning
  procedures. MWAA does not need to invent a physics chapter for its manual; it
  needs to adopt ACRP 175 by reference and set airport-specific numeric targets
  above the ACRP floor. [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/24839/improving-intelligibility-of-airport-terminal-public-address-systems]

- **NFPA 72 is the mandatory floor, and it is a low floor.** NFPA 72 (2022) Annex
  D states that intelligibility is acceptable if at least 90% of measurement
  locations in an "acoustically distinguishable space" (ADS) achieve STI ≥ 0.45,
  with an average ≥ 0.50 (equivalent to CIS 0.65 / 0.70). Crucially, NFPA 72
  treats quantitative measurement as *permitted, not required* — a listening test
  can satisfy the code. A MWAA standard that stops at NFPA compliance will get
  concourses that pass a listening test and still sound like every other American
  airport. [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/]

- **Silent-airport policy is nine to twenty-five years old, not new.** Schiphol
  branded itself a silent airport in the 2000s; Helsinki-Vantaa turned off
  concourse-wide flight and passenger announcements on 1 June 2015; London City
  operates a silent departure lounge with tannoy use restricted to emergencies
  and bad weather; SFO's Quiet Airport program (from 2018) reports a ~40%
  reduction in announcements and roughly 90 minutes of announcements per day
  eliminated in the International Terminal. [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]
  [Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]
  This is not experimental. It is a well-worn operating model.

- **Directional loudspeaker arrays are moving from novelty to mainline transport
  infrastructure.** Deutsche Bahn's Frankfurt Central Station trialed Holoplot's
  beamforming arrays to deliver platform-specific announcements without bleed
  into adjacent areas. In airport terms this is the technology that finally
  breaks the "one PA, one concourse" architecture and lets a single hold room be
  addressed without spilling into the gate next door. [Source: https://audioxpress.com/news/holoplot-showcases-broader-possibilities-for-3d-audio-beamforming-and-wavefield-synthesis-at-ise-2024]
  The technology is real; the cost is not "just another PA system."

- **Bluetooth LE Audio / Auracast is the accessibility inflection point.** In 2026
  Auracast is a shipping Bluetooth SIG standard, supported in shipping hearing
  aids from GN/ReSound, Starkey, and others. It replaces induction loops with
  software-defined broadcast zones and can deliver the *same* audio to a
  hearing-aid user, a passenger with earbuds, and an ADA assistive-listening
  receiver. This is the first credible way for a quiet terminal to reduce ambient
  announcements without cutting the deaf and hard-of-hearing population out of
  the information stream. [Source: https://www.bluetooth.com/auracast/assistive-listening/]

- **Sensory-inclusion "certification" is a training and signage program, not a
  technology stack.** KultureCity's Sensory Inclusive certification — deployed at
  LaGuardia Terminal B, Pittsburgh, Minneapolis-St Paul, and dozens of other
  airports — is primarily staff training plus sensory bags and a room. Airports
  buy it because it is credible and cheap, not because it changes the acoustic
  environment. Any MWAA quiet standard should treat it as a Layer 4
  complement, not a substitute for Layer 1 acoustic performance. [Source: https://www.aviationpros.com/airport-business/news/55337463/laguardia-terminal-b-introduces-new-sensory-accessibility-program-through-partnership-with-kulturecity]

- **The failure mode is announcement-count metrics.** SFO's own program measures
  itself in announcements-removed-per-day, which is a policy metric, not an
  acoustic one. Passenger complaints in the trade press about the SFO policy
  cluster around missed information, not decibels. [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]
  A MWAA standard measured in "announcements eliminated" will regress the same
  way. The right metrics are Layer 1 and Layer 2 (NC, RT, STI in each ADS) plus
  Layer 3/4 information-delivery SLAs (screen coverage per seat, app push latency,
  Auracast coverage of every gate), with announcement policy as an operational
  procedure downstream of those.

