## 3. Evidence — technology categories, standards, deployments

### 3.1 Standards catalog for a MWAA design manual

The following standards are what an actual quiet-by-design specification would
reference. All are real, current, and available to a MWAA design consultant.

- **NFPA 72 (2022), National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code**, Chapter 24 and
  Annex D — voice intelligibility, ADS definition, STI/CIS thresholds. Governs
  the *emergency* voice layer. [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/]
- **IEC 60268-16**, Sound system equipment — Objective rating of speech
  intelligibility by speech transmission index. The measurement method (STIPA)
  referenced by NFPA 72 and by ACRP 175. [Source: https://www.iso.org/standard/74237.html for related family; primary IEC document is print/paid]
- **EN 54-16**, Voice alarm control and indicating equipment. European
  equivalent; relevant if MWAA specifies EN-certified DSP for
  interoperability. [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/]
- **ISO 3382-3:2012**, Measurement of room acoustic parameters — Open plan
  offices. Directly applicable to airside concourses and check-in halls; defines
  distraction distance and spatial decay of speech, both of which map cleanly to
  terminal layout decisions. [Source: https://www.iso.org/standard/74237.html adjacent standard family]
- **ISO 22955:2021**, Acoustic quality of open office spaces. Not written for
  airports, but the only published standard that treats large open volumes as
  activity-typed zones with numeric acoustic targets; a useful template for the
  "acoustically distinguishable space" categories a MWAA standard would need.
  [Source: https://www.iso.org/standard/74237.html]
- **ANSI/ASA S12.2**, Criteria for evaluating room noise (NC / RC / NCB). The
  ambient sound criteria a MWAA standard would set per space type.
- **ADA 2010 Standards, §706** and **§219** — assistive listening: number of
  receivers required, hearing-aid compatibility. Sets the accessibility floor
  Auracast rollouts must meet or exceed.
- **ACRP Research Report 175 (2017)** — the airport-specific document; contains
  survey data, design guidelines, and a commissioning chapter that could be
  lifted almost verbatim into a MWAA design-manual appendix. [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/11]

### 3.2 Airports with documented deployments

- **Helsinki (HEL / Finavia)** — Silent airport since 1 June 2015. No terminal-
  wide flight or passenger announcements; passengers directed to FIDS, website,
  and app. Gate-side and emergency announcements retained. Quiet pods, quiet
  rooms, and a published sensory offer. [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]
  [Source: https://customerservice.finavia.fi/en/support/solutions/articles/79000120316-is-there-a-quiet-room-at-the-airport-]
- **Amsterdam Schiphol (AMS)** — "Virtually no announcements"; information is
  pushed to FIDS, website, and Schiphol app. Also runs a formal accessibility
  program for deaf and blind passengers alongside the silent policy. [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]
  [Source: https://www.schiphol.nl/en/assistance/flying-for-the-deaf-and-blind/]
- **London City (LCY)** — Silent departure lounge; tannoy only during emergency
  or weather disruption; passengers rely on FIDS. [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]
- **San Francisco (SFO)** — Quiet Airport since 2018. Music restrictions on
  retail tenants, boarding announcements confined to the immediate boarding
  area, no terminal-wide "final call." Reported ~40% announcement reduction and
  ~90 minutes/day removed in the International Terminal. Public reaction has
  been polarized; missed-information complaints are the dominant negative. [Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]
  [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]
- **LaGuardia Terminal B (LGA)** — KultureCity Sensory Inclusive certification;
  sensory bags, sensory room, staff training. Represents the training-and-
  facilities model rather than the acoustic model. [Source: https://www.aviationpros.com/airport-business/news/55337463/laguardia-terminal-b-introduces-new-sensory-accessibility-program-through-partnership-with-kulturecity]

### 3.3 Vendor landscape (2026, off-the-shelf)

- **IP-audio DSP / zone control platforms:** QSC Q-SYS, Biamp Tesira, Bosch
  Praesensa (EN 54-16), Bose ControlSpace, Ateis, TOA. Any of these can deliver
  Layer 2 zoning against an ACRP-175-quality design; the differentiator is EN 54
  certification (Praesensa) if MWAA wants a single certified emergency + PA
  platform.
- **Directional / beamformed arrays:** Holoplot X1/X2 (beamforming and wavefield
  synthesis; Frankfurt Central Station reference), Meyer Sound Panaray,
  Renkus-Heinz IC / Iconyx (steerable columns). Renkus-Heinz Iconyx is the
  price-per-coverage sweet spot for hold rooms with hard finishes. Holoplot is
  the ceiling of what is possible and prices accordingly. [Source: https://audioxpress.com/news/holoplot-showcases-broader-possibilities-for-3d-audio-beamforming-and-wavefield-synthesis-at-ise-2024]
- **STI/STIPA measurement:** NTi Audio XL2 with STIPA option, Bedrock Audio
  SM90, Bruel & Kjaer 2270. Any of these is capable of documenting NFPA 72
  Annex D compliance and ACRP 175 design values. MWAA should require quarterly
  measurement, not one-time commissioning.
- **Ambient sound monitoring dashboards:** Softdb, RION, and DIY LoRa/IP sensor
  meshes are all viable. This is now off-the-shelf; the question is who owns
  the alerts.
- **Auracast broadcasters:** Ampetronic (already the market leader in induction
  loops), Contacta, and a widening list of standard AV-over-IP vendors are
  shipping Auracast-capable transmitters in 2026. Hearing-aid support is
  shipping from GN/ReSound and others. [Source: https://www.bluetooth.com/auracast/assistive-listening/]
- **FIDS and digital wayfinding:** SITA, Amadeus, INFAX, Ultramain are the
  incumbent airport-specific vendors; digital signage is commoditized. Screen
  density per seat is the real MWAA design decision, not vendor selection.
- **Sensory certification:** KultureCity is the only widely recognized U.S.
  airport certification. Missoula, LGA Terminal B, and MSP are documented
  reference sites. [Source: https://opendoorsnfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/US-Airports-with-Sensory-Rooms.pdf]

### 3.4 Off-the-shelf vs custom in 2026

**Off-the-shelf in 2026:** EN 54-16 certified voice-alarm DSP; zone-per-gate
software control; STIPA measurement; commodity FIDS; KultureCity certification;
Auracast broadcasters at gate scale; steerable-column loudspeakers.

**Still custom / integration work:** whole-terminal acoustic modeling tied to the
architectural model (BIM-integrated ODEON or CATT-Acoustic runs); MWAA-specific
announcement scripting and category taxonomy; airline-app deep links for
individual boarding pushes at DCA/IAD; Auracast integration with airline
gate-hosting; PA/FIDS/app/Auracast governance policy (who can trigger what, on
which zones, under which operating scenarios); commissioning acceptance criteria
that survive a substantial-completion punch list.

### 3.5 Cost and ROI — what is known, what is claimed, what is not

Public airport disclosures rarely separate PA/FIDS/acoustic cost from the shell
they sit in, and vendor case studies almost never publish audited ROI. What is
defensible to say:

- ACRP 175 explicitly frames intelligibility as a design-and-commissioning
  problem, not a procurement one, and notes that "the technology to achieve
  appropriate acoustics is readily available" — i.e., the marginal capex is
  small when specified early and large when retrofitted. [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/11]
- SFO reports announcement-count reductions but does not publish independently
  audited passenger-experience or missed-boarding data tied to the Quiet Airport
  program. Read the 40% and 90-minute figures as self-reported policy metrics,
  not as measured outcomes. [Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]
- Vendor ROI claims for directional loudspeaker arrays (Holoplot and peers) are
  almost entirely drawn from live-events and rail deployments. There is no
  peer-reviewed airport ROI study in the public record.

Any cost range in a MWAA standard should therefore be built bottom-up from
per-square-foot loudspeaker density and finish upgrade allowances, not from
vendor case studies.

### 3.6 Failures and stalled deployments

The public record on failed quiet-airport deployments is thin because failures
are unbranded — an airport that quietly turned announcements back on rarely
publishes a case study. What is visible:

- **SFO policy backlash.** Passenger and aviation-press complaints about missed
  boardings and confusion at SFO are documented. The failure mode is treating
  announcement removal as the goal rather than as a downstream consequence of
  better information architecture. [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]
- **Silent-airport branding as a marketing exercise.** Multiple "silent airport"
  lists in the trade press include airports whose only implementation is a
  press release. A MWAA standard should refuse to accept "silent" as a claim
  without STI, RT, NC, and per-ADS measurement.
- **Induction-loop obsolescence risk.** Airports installing new copper induction
  loops in 2026, with Auracast shipping in mainstream hearing aids, face a
  written-down asset within a design life.

