## 5. Verbatim data points a strategist can use

1. "The intelligibility of an emergency communication system is considered
   acceptable if at least 90 percent of the measurement locations within each
   ADS have a measured Speech Transmission Index (STI) of not less than 0.45
   (0.65 CIS) and an average STI of not less than 0.50 STI (0.70 CIS)."
   — NFPA 72 Annex D, as quoted by industry compliance summaries. [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/]

2. "The airport advertises that these new policies have reduced the number of
   announcements by 40% in boarding areas. In the International Terminal
   they've eliminated over 90 minutes of unnecessary announcements a day."
   — SFO Quiet Airport program, self-reported. [Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]

3. Helsinki-Vantaa became a silent airport on 1 June 2015 — "no flight or
   passenger announcements will be made in the terminal," with exceptions
   retained for gate-side changes, disruption, and FIDS failure. [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]

4. London City Airport policy: "No tannoy announcements are made throughout the
   departure lounge. Only in the case of emergency or during bad weather will
   announcements be made." — LCY Silent Airport Policy page. [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]

5. "Acoustics is often called the forgotten dimension in architectural design"
   and "the technology to achieve appropriate acoustics is readily available."
   — ACRP Research Report 175, foreword. [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/11]

