## 6. Implications for the MWAA standard

Three implications flow from the technology scan:

**Specify performance, name standards, avoid product names.** The MWAA design
manual should adopt NFPA 72 Annex D as the emergency floor, adopt ACRP 175 as
the design-and-commissioning method, adopt ISO 22955 (or its logic) for ADS
categorization, and set per-ADS NC / RT / STI targets that are more demanding
than the NFPA floor. It should not name vendors.

**Require Layer 4 accessibility before permitting Layer 2 reduction.** Any
reduction in ambient announcements at a MWAA gate should be gated on Auracast or
equivalent broadcast coverage plus ADA §706 compliance plus documented
airline-app push capability. This is the language that keeps a quiet-terminal
standard from becoming an accessibility regression.

**Build a measurement regime, not a policy count.** Quarterly STI/STIPA
measurement per ADS, published; ambient NC monitoring against a live baseline;
missed-boarding rates by gate benchmarked against pre-standard baseline. If the
standard cannot be measured against these, it will drift into "fewer
announcements" and inherit SFO's backlash.

The Council's larger question — what is mandatory across MWAA, what varies by
airport, what varies by terminal — has a clean technology-side answer.
Mandatory: Layer 1 acoustic performance targets, NFPA 72 compliance envelope,
STI measurement regime, Auracast/ADA §706 accessibility floor, ACRP 175
commissioning. Airport-specific: PA governance policy (DCA's slot-constrained
short-journey operation cannot tolerate the same announcement minimalism as
IAD's international transfer hall). Context-dependent: directional array
deployment (justified in acoustically hostile hold rooms, not everywhere) and
Layer 3 screen density (a function of terminal geometry). That is the shape of a
standard the design manual can actually enforce.
